Provenance and licence of the DeltaBlue program
===============================================

DeltaBlue is John Maloney's constraint-solver benchmark. These files are ports of the
JavaScript version in Are We Fast Yet (Stefan Marr, Benoit Daloze and Hanspeter Mössenböck,
https://github.com/smarr/are-we-fast-yet, benchmarks/JavaScript/deltablue.js, fetched
2026-10-18), which says of itself:

    The benchmark in its current state is a derivation from the SOM version,
    which is derived from Mario Wolczko's Smalltalk version of DeltaBlue.

    The original license details are available here:
    http://web.archive.org/web/20050825101121/http://www.sunlabs.com/people/mario/java_benchmarking/index.html

and whose LICENSE.md says, for Richards and DeltaBlue:

    These benchmark are derived from the Smalltalk sources provided by Mario Wolczko.
    License details are available at:
      http://web.archive.org/web/20050825101121/http://www.sunlabs.com/people/mario/java_benchmarking/index.html
    Further information:
      http://www.wolczko.com/java_benchmarking.html

The archived page credits John Maloney "for creating the DeltaBlue benchmark, and granting
permission to distribute a translation of the benchmark" and links its licensing terms from a
separate page that was not retrieved for this commit. A maintainer must confirm those terms
before this program is redistributed outside this repository.

Changes from the Are We Fast Yet version
----------------------------------------

js/main.mjs keeps AWFY's classes and methods. sakura/main.sak reimplements the same
algorithm over persistent lists of variable and constraint records indexed by dense id,
because Sakura Lang has no mutable objects; every traversal and queue keeps AWFY's order. Both:

- replace SOM's Vector with lists/arrays; queues advance a head index (SOM's removeFirst is
  O(1)); removing a constraint from a variable drops every occurrence, as SOM's remove does;
- sort the unsatisfied constraints strongest first with a stable sort instead of SOM's
  in-place sort (Array.prototype.sort; List.sort_by);
- use integer values: ScaleConstraint's backward step is Int division in Sakura and `/` in
  JS, which agree because every division in the two tests is exact (the oracle checks it);
- return a checksum instead of `true`: chainTest returns the sum of the 100 values it checks
  plus the plan length; projectionTest returns the two checked values plus the sums of all
  destination values after the scale and offset changes, and 0 for n = 0 (AWFY's version
  needs n >= 1). run(n) adds the two. Every AWFY check still fails the run (a defect in
  Sakura, an Error in JS).

Owner acceptance: @icantcodefyi accepted including this port under the terms above on
2026-10-18.
